Uniform Phase Permutation of Efficient Ruddlesden–Popper Perovskite Solar Cells via Binary Spacers and Single Crystal Coordination
2D Ruddlesden-Popper perovskites (RPPs) have attracted extensive attention in recent years due to their excellent environmental stability. However, the power conversion efficiency (PCE) of RPP solar cells is much lower than that of 3D perovskite solar cells (PSCs), mainly attributed to their poor carrier transport performance and excessive heterogeneous phases. Herein, the binary spacers (n-butylammonium, BA and benzamidine, PFA) are introduced to regulate the crystallization kinetics and \( n \)-value phase distribution to form uniform phase permutation of RPP films. The study then incorporates \( n=5 \) \( \mathsf{B A}_{2}\mathsf{M A}_{4}\mathsf{P b}_{5}\mathsf{I}_{16} \) memory single crystal to achieve ultrafast stepped-type carrier transport from the low \( n \)-value phases to the high \( n \)-value phases in the high-quality \( (\mathsf{B A}_{0.75}\mathsf{P F A}_{0.25})_{2}\mathsf{M A}_{4}\mathsf{P b}_{5}\mathsf{I}_{16} \) films. These binary spacers and single-crystal-assisted crystallization strategies produce high-quality films, leading to fast carrier extraction and significant nonradiative recombination suppression. The resulting PSC presents a champion PCE of \( 21.15\% \) with an impressive open circuit voltage \( (V_{\infty}) \) of \( 1.26\,\mathsf{V} \), which is the record high efficiency and \( V_{\infty} \) for low \( n \)-value RPP solar cells \( (n\leq5) \).
## 1. Introduction
Organic–inorganic hybrid perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have reached a power conversion efficiency (PCE) of \( 26.7\% \) owing to their low defect density, low exciton binding energy, excellent charge transfer, and bandgap tunability.[1–6] However, the degradation of \( \mathsf{M A P b I}_{3} \) (methylammonium, MA) and the phase transition of \( \alpha \)-FAPbI\(_3\) (formal cadmium, FA) has seriously hindered the commercialization of PSCs.[7,8] In contrast, 2D Ruddlesden–Popper perovskites (RPPs) have attracted great attention because of their superior structural and environmental stability deriving from the hydrophobic organic spacer cations and high formation energy.[9,10] The formula structure of RPPs can be defined as \( (\mathbf{A}^{\prime\prime})_{2}(\mathbf{A})_{n-1}\mathbf{M}_{n}\mathbf{X}_{3n+1} \), where \( \mathbf{A}^{\prime\prime} \) is a large aliphatic or aromatic alkylammonium
spacer cation intercalating between inorganic frameworks, A is a monovalent organic cation, M is a divalent metal cation, X is a halide anion, as well as \( n \) represents the layer number of the inorganic slabs.[11,12] However, embedding organic spacer cation produces a dielectric confinement effect and leads to electron-hole pairs being trapped in quantum wells (QWs). The exciton binding energy of RPP films is much higher than that of 3D counterparts, which is not favorable for exciton dissociation in the electrical field.[13] In addition, the disordered phase distribution of \( n \) values in the RPP thin films induces undesired energy transfer losses, limiting the open circuit voltage \( \left(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\right) \) and PCE of solar cells.[14]
To regulate the exciton binding energy of RPP films, the rational design of organic spacers by constructing special structural spacers and introducing binary spacers are investigated. In the past decades, the low \( n \)-value RPP optoelectronic devices \( (n \leq 5) \) have shown great potential based on a variety of novel organic spacers, compared to traditional butylammonium (BA) and phenethylammonium (PEA) spacers. For instance, the various MA derivatives-based organic spacers were employed in high-performance PSCs due to their unique properties.[15–18] Meanwhile, the FA derivatives-based organic spacers were also successfully applied to fabricate efficient and stable RPP optoelectronic devices.[19,20] Further, the azetidine-based secondary organic spacer, 3,3-difluoroazetidine, can be used as one of the spacer cations to prepare PSCs with a PCE of \( 19.85\% \) \( (n = 4) \).[21] Specifically, interactions between different electronegative spacers and interlayer interactions in binary spacers can regulate the spacer assembly and crystallization processes.[22] Compared to unary spacers, binary spacers have great potential since the strong interactions between two organic spacers could modulate crystal growth with preferential orientation, and reduce exciton binding energy. Zhou et al. introduced a binary spacer \( \left(\mathrm{BA}_{0.9}\mathrm{PEA}_{0.1}\right) \), which significantly improved the \( V_{\mathrm{OC}} \) of the device. Introducing relatively "rigid" PEA spacers reduces the exciton binding energy and modulates the carrier transport.[23] Chen et al. exploited quadrupole-quadrupole interactions to regulate the nucleation process of inclusion crystals by introducing perfluorobenzothiophene-methylamine and benzothiophene-methylamine organic spacers with different electronegativities, achieving a champion efficiency of \( 18.56\% \) \( (n = 5) \).[22] Accompanied by reduced nucleation sites and facilitated formation of compact grains, the incorporation of binary spacers can achieve high-quality thin films to enhance the performance of PSCs.[24]
Currently, 3D perovskite films based on a mixture of MA and FA A-site cations have shown excellent photovoltaic properties, which are attributed to longer carrier diffusion lengths and significantly lower trap state densities. Despite the expansion of novel spacers, based on MA derivatives and FA derivatives binary spacers remain unexplored in RPP devices. Additionally, the crystal orientation distribution, crystallization process, and phase distribution play a crucial role in high-quality films and excellent device performance.[25–27] At present, some methods such as hot casting and the introduction of additives have been developed to successfully prepare vertically oriented RPP films.[28] In particular, Shao et al. achieved a champion PCE of \( 21.07\% \) based on the MACl and \( \mathrm{PbCl}_{2} \) additives, which is the highest efficiency available for low \( n \)-value RPP devices \( (n \leq 5) \).[29] For regulating the phase distribution and crystallization strategy, Zhang et al. currently proposed the construction of vertically ordered dispersion films based on vacuum poling, as well as Yue et al. introduced an inorganic co-solvent.[30,31] Sidhik et al. fabricated high-purity 2D perovskite films by introducing single-crystal with memory function as crystalline species and regulating nucleation.[32] Based on the single-crystal coordination strategy, we find that memory single-crystal exhibits excellent phase regulation. Moreover, the binary spacers are favorable for lowering the exciton binding energy because of the interactions between spacers with different electronegativity. However, the incorporation of memory single-crystal for phase regulation and crystal growth in RPP films mixed with binary spacers of MA and FA derivatives has seldom been investigated.
In this study, the binary spacers of MA-derivative BA and FA-derivative benzamidine (PFA) were employed for high-performance single crystal incorporated \( \left(\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25}\right)_{2}\mathrm{MA}_{4}\mathrm{Pb}_{5}\mathrm{I}_{16} \) \( (n = 5) \) PSCs. It demonstrated that incorporating the "rigid" PFA spacers reduces the exciton binding energy of the film, and regulates the vertical orientation. With \( \mathrm{BA}_{2}\mathrm{MA}_{4}\mathrm{Pb}_{5}\mathrm{I}_{16} \) memory single crystal, the crystal orientation distribution, crystallization process, and phase distribution were modulated for the ultra-fast carrier transport of charge carriers from the low \( n \) phases to the high \( n \) phases. Compared to traditional films, single crystal incorporated \( \left(\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25}\right)_{2}\mathrm{MA}_{4}\mathrm{Pb}_{5}\mathrm{I}_{16} \) films have a preferential orientation, low nonradiative recombination defects, and a long carrier lifetime. Inspiringly, the p-i-n inverted solar cell based on this modified film achieved a recorded \( V_{\mathrm{OC}} \) of \( 1.26\ \mathrm{V} \) and PCE of \( 21.15\% \), which is the highest efficiency so far for RPP devices with low \( n \) values \( (n \leq 5) \). Importantly, compared to 3D counterparts, the \( \left(\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25}\right) \)-based RPP devices using single crystal coordinated exhibits superior environmental stability due to the hydrophobicity nature of organic spacer cation and high formation energy. The PCE of single crystal incorporated \( \left(\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25}\right)_{2}\mathrm{MA}_{4}\mathrm{Pb}_{5}\mathrm{I}_{16} \) PSCs without encapsulation retained \( 95\% \) of its original value after \( 3000\ \mathrm{h} \) of storage under \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) conditions.
## 2. Results and Discussion
The precursor solution of \( \left(\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25}\right)_{2}\mathrm{MA}_{4}\mathrm{Pb}_{5}\mathrm{I}_{16} \) \( (n = 5) \) was prepared by dissolving BAI, PFACl molar ratio of 3:1). Additionally, we synthesized a \( \mathrm{BA}_{2}\mathrm{MA}_{4}\mathrm{Pb}_{5}\mathrm{I}_{16} \) \( (n = 5) \) single crystal by cooling crystallization to enhance crystallization as shown in
Figure 1a. The single crystal of \(\mathrm{BA}_{2}\mathrm{MA}_{4}\mathrm{Pb}_{5}\mathrm{I}_{16}\) shows a clear laminar distribution on the \(100~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) scale, consistent with the previous report.[33] The photographs and X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns for \(\mathrm{BA}_{2}\mathrm{MA}_{4}\mathrm{Pb}_{5}\mathrm{I}_{16}\) \((n=5)\) single crystals are shown in Figures S1, S2 (Supporting Information). We spin-coated RPP films by using the antisolvent method. XRD patterns were utilized to investigate the effects of PFA serving as \(1/4\) spacer and \(\mathrm{BA}_{2}\mathrm{MA}_{4}\mathrm{Pb}_{5}\mathrm{I}_{16}\) single crystals \((n=5)\) on the crystallinity of RPP films. In Figure 1b, the two predominant diffraction peaks were observed at \({\approx}14.2^{\circ}\) and \(28.5^{\circ}\) for all samples, assigned to the (111) and (202) lattice planes, respectively.[28] Meanwhile, the incorporation of PFA spacers induces main diffraction peaks to shift toward higher diffraction angles, showing a reduction in the crystallographic interplanar spacing and the lattice contraction attributed to lattice distortion.[34] Compared to the control films, the single crystal incorporated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based film shows the strongest diffraction intensity, indicating its superior crystallinity. Generally, the (202) and (111) diffraction peaks of the RPP films represent the crystallographic planes of the inorganic layer aligning vertically and tilted to the substrate, respectively. The XRD
diffraction peak (202)/(111) intensity ratio enhances from 0.46 to 0.55 and 0.63 in the BA-, \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) - and the single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based film, which indicates that the single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based film exhibit higher vertical orientation than that of BA-based film.[15,25]
Figure 1c represents the ultraviolet-visible (UV-vis) absorption spectra of various films. All films exhibit multiple excitonic peaks at \({\approx}605\), 641, and \(670\,\mathrm{\nm}\) corresponding to \(n\,=\,3\), 4, and 5 phases.[35] Especially, the \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) - and the single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based films show stronger absorption peaks than that of the BA-based film at \(750\,\mathrm{nm}\) corresponding to 3D phases. In the top excitation (film side), the steady-state photoluminescence (PL) intensity in Figure 1d indicates that the introduction of the PFA spacer effectively modulates the bandgap of perovskite films. The BA-, \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) - and single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based RPPs dominate the PL emission peaks at 756, 768, and \(768\,\mathrm{\nm}\), respectively, assigned to the 3D phase. The predominant peaks of \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based and single crystal coordination \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based films have slightly redshift indicating a decrease in bandgap and exciton
binding energy. The redshift of the PL emission peaks of the films doped with PFA spacers suggests that the PFA spacer has been inserted into the organic spacer layer. Notably, the insertion of the PFA spacer with a large dipole moment into the control film leads to a decrease in the distance between the nitrogen cation and the iodide, allowing for an increase in NH···I hydrogen bonding interactions.[22,34,36] Additionally, the \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) - and single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based films exhibit a stronger PL peak intensity than that of BA-based film, suggesting the better film quality and the lower nonradiative recombination loss.[37] To further analyze the phase distribution in the vertical substrate direction, the normalized PL intensities of the dual-side were measured. In the top (film side) and bottom (glass side) excited PL spectra (Figure 1e,f), the multiple higher energy peaks positioned at 614, 650, and \(673\,\mathrm{nm}\) in BA-based films were observed, arising from emissions of \(n=3\) , 4, and 5 phases, respectively. Compared to BA-based films, the 2D phase contents of \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) - and single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) - based films are significantly reduced. Figure S3a–c (Supporting Information) shows the PL spectra of the various films excited from the top and bottom, revealing the spatial arrangement of different phases. Specifically, the less difference in the positions of the PL emission peaks between the \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) - and single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based films on both the film and substrate sides indicate a uniform phase distribution of different \(n\) values in the two samples, with a high \(n\) phase being the main component. The formation of such a uniform phase distribution films is attributed to the phase modulation function of the "rigid" PFA spacer, resulting in schematic models of the phase distribution of the three 2D RPP films (Figure S4, Supporting Information). The low \(n\) phases dominant in the BA-based film are mainly located on the front side (film side), exhibiting disordered spatial phase distribution, significantly hindering vertical charge carrier transport. Conversely, the \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) - and single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based films are dominated by the high \(n\) phase, showing an ordered spatial arrangement in the vertical direction, which is beneficial for carrier transport.
To further observe the effects of PFA spacer and single crystal on RPP film crystallization, the morphology and coverage were investigated using scanning electron microscopy (SEM), as shown in Figure 1g–i. Compared to the BA films, \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) - and single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based films show a compact and uniform morphology. Specifically, no significant laminar distribution was observed in the single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based films, which favors carrier transport. In Figure S5 (Supporting Information), the presence of needle-shaped crystals in the BA-based films may be due to the formation of intermediate phases during crystal growth, suggesting a disordered phase distribution. In addition, the lattice constants of the \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) - and single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based films change because of the insertion of the PFA spacer layer, resulting in a wrinkle texture on the surface.[38] To some extent, the wrinkles texture on the surface facilitates the formation of a denser and more uniform film structure. The single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based films exhibit an inconspicuous wrinkles texture on the surface while maintaining a dense film, indicating their low defect density and excellent film quality. The BA-based films exhibit amounts of apparent cracks, which can lead to severe defect recombination losses
and are detrimental to carrier transport (Figure S6, Supporting Information).
To evaluate the carrier dynamics and spatial phase distribution in various RPP films, the chirp-corrected femtosecond transient absorption (TA) spectroscopy was monitored. Upon bottom excitation with a \(470\ \mathrm{nm}\) pulse, the carrier transfer behaviors and ground-states photobleaching (PB) signals of 2D phases in the RPP films are shown in Figure 2a–c. The PB peaks of 2D phases appeared \({\approx}606\) , 641, and \(668~\mathrm{nm}\) , representing \(n\,=\,3,\,4\) , and 5 phases in the three RPP films, respectively, in agreement with the UV-vis absorption spectra and the steady-state PL spectra.[39] The signals of the high \(n\) phase PB peaks of \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) - and single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based films gradually enhanced compared to the BA-based films, indicating a higher content of high \(n\) phase and suppression of low \(n\) phase. In particular, the single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based film exhibits an orderly stepped type from the low \(n\) phase to the high \(n\) phase, which reduces the energy transfer losses due to disordered phase distribution. As shown in the TA spectra at the selected delay time (Figure 2d–f), the PB peaks appear in the \(n\,=\) 3, 4, and 5 phases in the three RPP films. Notably, the PB signals of the 3D phase for the \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) - and single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based films exhibit significantly redshift compared to the BA-based films. The apparent redshift of the 3D phase absorption peak was mainly attributed to the incorporation of PFA which reduced the exciton binding energy of the films. Like PL spectra, the full width at half maximum (FWHM) of PB peaks in the 3D phase significantly narrows, attributed to low band tail trap density in the \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) - and single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based films. Because of the memory function of the single crystal, the single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based films exhibit an ordered and fast step-type charge transfer from the low \(n\) phase to the high \(n\) phase, effectively reducing energy loss and non-radiative recombination.
To evaluate the carrier/exciton transfer dynamic among the various phases, decay time-dependent TA signals at selected PB resonances were investigated (Figure 2g–i), and the exponential fitting parameters are shown in Table S1 (Supporting Information), where rise time \(\tau_{1}\) , fast decay \(\tau_{2}\) , and slow decay \(\tau_{3}\) , correspond to the fast establishment of the PB peaks, the charge transfer, and the charge recombination process, respectively.[40] With the increase of \(n\) value, the decay dynamics of the various films slows down. The carrier population time of the BA-based, \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based and single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based films decrease progressively in the rising part of the PB peak at the 3D phase to \({\approx}2.0\) , 1.5, and 1.1 ps, respectively. We also evaluated the fast decay time \(\tau_{2}\) of the PB peak corresponding to \(n=5\) for the BA-based \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based and single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based films, which are extracted to be 45.78, 47.84, and 8.27 ps, respectively. The shortest fast decay time \(\tau_{2}\) of the \(n=5\) phase, along with the fastest build-up time of the establishment of the 3D phase, indicate the most efficient charge transfer from the low \(n\) phase to the high \(n\) phase in the single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based film. The reduction in charge transfer time can be attributed to the orderly phase arrangement and the induction of hydrogen bonds between organic spacers, the schematic is shown in Figure S4 (Supporting Information). Thus, introducing PFA spacers and
single crystals effectively enhances the charge transfer, which can improve the performance of the device.[41]
The crystal orientation and phase distribution of the three RPP films were analyzed via grazing-incidence wide-angle Xray scattering (GIWAXS) measurements. As shown in Figure 3a–c, the (111) plane tilted to the substrate and the (202) plane vertical to the substrate in the RPP film are located at \(q\approx1.0{-}2.0\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}\), respectively. In particular, the crystal diffraction of the (0k0) plane of \(q_{z}\approx0.25{-}0.5\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}\) confirmed the presence of low \(n\) phases in the three RPP films.[42,43] Compared to the BA-based films, the \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\)-based films show weaker diffraction intensity of the 2D counterpart (0k0) crystal planes. Furthermore, integration of the GWIAXS maps of the three films and comparison with their XRD patterns show that the (202)/(111) ratio overall has the same trend as the XRD diffraction peaks. For the BA-, \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\)- and the single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\)-based film, the GIWAXS diffraction peak (202)/(111) intensity ratio enhanced from 0.48 to 0.54 and 0.55 (Figure S7, Supporting Information). The introduction of "rigid" PFA spacers enhances the vertical orientation of the
crystals while effectively suppressing the low \(n\) phase, which facilitates carrier transport. For the single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\)-based films, the suppressed diffraction intensity of the (111) plane and the almost absent crystal diffraction intensity of the (0k0) plane verify the excellent vertical orientation and the modulation of the phase. The crystal diffraction intensity of the (0k0) plane gradually decreases from BA- to \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\)-to single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\)-based film. This suggests that PFA spacers and single crystals suppress the low \(n\) phases parallel to the substrate.
To further elucidate the influence of the PFA spacers and single crystal on the crystallization process, we conducted in situ steady-state PL and UV-vis absorption spectra measurements for the one-step spin-coating and thermal annealing processes, respectively (Figure 3d–i). With the start of the spin-coating procedure, the PL signals for all three films appeared at \(\approx20\,s\), the drip time of the antisolvent chlorobenzene, suggesting that the antisolvent promoted perovskites crystallization (Figure 3d–f). For \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\)- and single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\)-based films, similar to the steady-state PL spectra, the FWHM of
the PL peaks during in situ spin-coating was narrower, indicating a low trap density. It is noteworthy that although the BA-based film showed a stronger PL intensity than the \((\mathsf{B A}_{0.75}\mathsf{P F A}_{0.25})\) - based film, the PL intensity suddenly became weaker after \(27\,\mathrm{s}\) . The BA-based films exhibit disorder during crystal growth, which unfavorably contributed to the formation of a uniform, highquality film. The high PL intensity and good crystallization quality of the single crystal coordinated \((\mathsf{B A}_{0.75}\mathsf{P F A}_{0.25})\) -based films compared to the pure \((\mathsf{B A}_{0.75}\mathsf{P F A}_{0.25})\) -based films attributed to the induced effect of the single crystals. As shown in the TA spectra and XRD, the single crystals make the phase distribution stepped, which accelerates the carrier transport from the low \(n\) phase to the high \(n\) phase and exhibits a superior crystallinity. It can also be seen by the UV-vis absorption spectra of the in situ spin-coating that the single crystal coordinated \((\mathsf{B A}_{0.75}\mathsf{P F A}_{0.25})\) - based films show the strongest absorbance and the best crystalline quality (Figure S8, Supporting Information).
Unlike the rapid crystallization (7–10 s) of BA-based films, the \((\mathsf{B A}_{0.75}\mathsf{P F A}_{0.25})\) - and single crystal coordinated \((\mathsf{B A}_{0.75}\mathsf{P F A}_{0.25})\) - based films form seeds and grow slowly (0–18 s) during the annealing process, which tends to result in compactness, pinhole
Unlike the rapid crystallization (7–10 s) of BA-based films, the \((\mathsf{B A}_{0.75}\mathsf{P F A}_{0.25})\) - and single crystal coordinated \((\mathsf{B A}_{0.75}\mathsf{P F A}_{0.25})\) - based films form seeds and grow slowly (0–18 s) during the annealing process, which tends to result in compactness, pinhole-free, and uniform of films (Figure 3g–i). In particular, the PFA spacer was less soluble compared to the higher solubility BA spacer. For precursors doped with PFA spacers, the lower solubility PFA spacers precipitated and formed clusters due to supersaturation in the initial nucleation stage. We hypothesize that the clusters will act as seeds after nucleation and reduce nucleation sites to assist the crystallization of perovskite, which is favorable for the formation of large \(n\) -value phases and compactness film.[24,29,44] Comparatively, the pure BA-based films with higher solubility begin to crystallize rapidly at the 7th second of thermal annealing, with more nucleation sites presence, which is not conducive to the formation of large \(n\) -value phases and compactness.
To evaluate the interaction of the BA and PFA organic spacers as well as the interaction of the organic spacer and the inorganic \([\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}\) layer, we performed X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) on the films. As shown in Figure 4a,b and Figure S9 (Supporting Information), the introduction of the PFA spacer shifted the XPS peaks of both I 3d and Pb 4f toward higher binding energies, attributing to the change in the chemical environment of the \([\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}\) octahedron. Due to the addition of
the PFA spacer, the \(\mathrm{NH\cdotsI}\) hydrogen bonding interaction between the inorganic perovskite layer spacer and the inorganic \([\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}\) layer is enhanced, resulting in the peaks of I 3d and Pb 4f shifting toward higher binding energy compared to the control perovskite film. Interestingly, the N1s peaks exhibit an additional XPS peak at \(400.0\,\mathrm{\eV},\) confirming the presence of the PFA spacer in the \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) - and single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based films (Figure 4c). The results indicate that the introduction of PFA spacers increases the distortion of the Pb-I framework, which would reduce the interlayer space, thereby enhancing the strong \(\mathrm{NH\cdotsI}\) hydrogen bonding between the spacer and the inorganic \([\mathrm{PbI}_{6}]^{4-}\) layer.[45] This is consistent with the shift of the main diffraction peak toward a higher \(2\theta\) angle in the XRD patterns in the \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) - and single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based films.[17,18,46] The atomic force microscopy (AFM) images in Figure 3d–f, Figures S10, S11 (Supporting Information) show that the \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) - and the single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based films exhibit excellent film quality, uniform size distribution, uniform
morphology, and low surface roughness, with root mean square (RMS) roughness of only 16.8 and \(16.4\ \mathrm{nm}\) , in comparison to the BA-based film \((\mathrm{RMS}\,=\,39.5\ \mathrm{nm})\) ).[47] The incorporation of PFA spacers and single-crystal additives resulted in a denser and smoother surface morphology with significantly fewer holes. Kelvin Probe Force Microscopy (KPPM) in the same area characterization was then employed to investigate the surface potential distribution of the films. The Figure \(^{3\mathrm{g},\mathrm{h}}\) and Figure S12 (Supporting Information) show that the \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) - and the single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based films exhibit homogeneous surface potential distribution and lower potential barrier. For the surface potential at the grain boundaries, the BA-based films exhibit a larger surface potential difference of \({\approx}0.15\ \mathrm{~V~}\) compared to the \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) - and the single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\) -based films \((\approx\!0.05\mathrm{~V})\) , which severely hinders carrier transport, as shown in Figure S13 (Supporting Information). This result explains the film defect-passivation by PFA spacers with single crystals.[48]
We fabricated RPP solar cells with an inverted device structure of ITO/SAM ((2-(4-(Bis(4-methoxyphenyl)amino) phenyl)−1-cyanovinyl)phosphonic acid, MPA-CPA)/RPP/ PEACl/PCBM+PMMA/LiF/C60/BCP/Ag (Figure 5a). The SAM-based hole transport layer for 2D inverted devices significantly improved wettability and thermal stability compared to the conventional poly[bis(4-phenyl)(2,4,6-trimethylphenyl)amine] (PTAA) material. We investigated the wettability of perovskite precursors on the surface of two different hole transport layers, PTAA and MPA-CPA, by testing the contact angle. As shown in Figure S14 (Supporting Information), the MPA-CPA cavity transport layer exhibits strong wettability \((12.2^{\circ})\) compared to PTAA \((43.0^{\circ})\). The significant difference in contact angle is attributed to the introduction of the multifunctional cyanovinyl phosphonic acid group. Compared to conventional SAMs, MPA-CPA exhibits better hydrophilicity and defect passivation attributed to the synergistic coordination of the cyanide and phosphine groups with the lead ions. The current density-voltage \((J\!\!\!/-V)\) image in Figure 5b shows that the single crystal coordinated \((\mathsf{B A}_{0.75}\,\mathsf{P F A}_{0.25})\) -based device exhibits an exceptional PCE of \(21.15\%\), with a \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(1.26\,\mathrm{~V~}\), a short-circuit current density \((I_{\mathrm{SC}})\) of \(22.38\,\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}\), and a fill factor (FF) of \(75.20\%\) (the detailed data as shown in Table 1). The single crystal coordinated \((\mathsf{B A}_{0.75}\,\mathsf{P F A}_{0.25})\) -based device exhibited negligible hysteresis (Figure S15, Supporting Information). The BA-based, \((\mathsf{B A}_{0.75}\,\mathsf{P F A}_{0.25})\) -based and single crystal coordinated \((\mathsf{B A}_{0.75}\,\mathsf{P F A}_{0.25})\) -based device PCE and \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) distributions are shown in Figure S16 (Supporting Information). To the best of our knowledge, this sets a new record for low \(n\) RPP devices \((n\,\leq\,5)\) PCE. Figure 5c summarises the PCE of low \(n\) RPP devices \((n\,\leq\,5)\) based on different spacers in recent years, as shown in Table S2 (Supporting Information). The integrated \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) values from the external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra (Figure 5d) are 22.20, 21.82, \(19.89\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\) for the single crystal
