\[
\tau=\gamma_{0}+A_{1}\exp\left(\frac{-t}{\tau_{1}}\right)+A_{2}\exp\left(\frac{-t}{\tau_{2}}\right)
\]
Information) show that the \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\)- and single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\)-based devices retained \(\approx76\%\) and \(\approx91\%\) of their original PCE, respectively, compared to the BA-based devices that retained \(51\%\) of the original PCE after \(\approx850\,\mathrm{h}\). More surprisingly, after heating the device at \(85\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for over \(260\,\mathrm{h}\), the PCE maintains \(96.7\%\) (Figure S18, Supporting Information) of its original value for single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\)-based devices, which indicates extremely good thermal stability. The significant improvement in the environmental stability and thermal stability of \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\)-based and single crystal coordination \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\)-based films can be attributed to higher crystallinity and fewer defects compared to BA-based films. Water contact angle measurements were performed to investigate the hydrophobic nature of the three films. In particular, as illustrated in Figure S19 (Supporting Information), the single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\)-based film and \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\)-based film exhibits a larger water contact angle \((69.0^{\circ}\) and \(68.9^{\circ})\) than the BA-based film \((56.2^{\circ})\), indicating a relatively high water-resistant performance. The enhanced hydrophobicity nature of the films can be attributed to the improved film quality and the incorporation of "rigid" PFA hydrophobic organic cations. The stability track of the three devices under continuous illumination is shown in Figure S20 (Supporting Information). The improved photostability could benefit from the enhanced crystal quality and reduced defects in the single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\)-based film. After \(260\,\mathrm{h}\) continuous illumination, the PCE of single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\)-based devices only attenuates \(\approx2\%\) of the initial efficiency.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we explored the crystallization and carrier transport process of \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\)-based films \((n=5)\) with the uniform \(n\)-value phase dispersion by introducing single-crystal coordination. The \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\)-based and single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\)-based film \((n=5)\) exhibits improved quality, enlarged carrier lifetime, and a lower exciton binding energy compared to the BA-based film, attributed to the strong hydrogen bonding between the PFA spacer and inorganic \([\mathrm{PbX}_{6}]^{4-}\) octahedron layers. Importantly, the single crystal coordinated \((\mathrm{BA}_{0.75}\mathrm{PFA}_{0.25})\)-based film achieves an orderly stepped-type arrangement between different \(n\) phases, which greatly improves the carrier transport performance and achieves a recorded \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(1.26\,\mathrm{V}\) and PCE of \(21.15\%\). This work provides a viable approach for tailoring RPP devices by designing binary spacers containing MA derivatives and FA derivatives-based, as well as especially introducing memory functional \(\mathrm{BA}_{2}\mathrm{MA}_{4}\mathrm{Pb}_{516}\) \((n=5)\) single crystal seeds, offering excellent carrier transport properties and low nonradiative recombination loss of RPP device.
